description:
FILED s514d (Opus, 2026-10-10) from the CPAN board after #2590: Capture-Tiny's t/13/14/15/23-*-tied.t now run their TIEHANDLE, and t/lib/TieLC.pm's constructor dies.

A blessed GLOB reference that is then OPENED loses its class for method calls: the call dispatches to IO::Handle.

Reproducer (perl 5.40.3 prints `K K::hello` twice; PCL prints it once, then dies `Can't locate object method "hello" via package "IO::Handle"`):

    package K; sub hello { "K::hello" }
    package main;
    my $fh = \do { local *HANDLE };
    bless $fh, 'K';
    print ref($fh), " ", $fh->hello, "\n";
    open($fh, '<', $0) or die;
    print ref($fh), " ", $fh->hello, "\n";

TieLC (`my $fh = \do { local *HANDLE }; bless $fh, $class; $fh->OPEN(@_); $fh->BINMODE(':utf8')`) is the classic self-tied-handle-object idiom (IO::String, Tie::Handle subclasses).  Board effect: those four files read FAIL before (the tie was ignored: 1 / 1 / 1 / 3 failing rows) and FAIL after (no TAP rows: the TIEHANDLE dies); 24-all-badtied.t went FAIL -> PASS 3/3 in the same batch.  Suspect: the method-call invocant resolution checks "is an open filehandle -> IO::Handle" before "is blessed" for a box holding a glob that open() turned into a stream.
